\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces A taxonomy of cloud-native anomaly-detection approaches---from statistical and threshold rules and classical machine learning, through the deep-learning families (reconstruction, sequential and graph models), to LLM-based and multimodal MELT-fusion detectors---with representative works. The strongest recent results cluster at the multimodal end (shaded). Cutting \emph  {across} this technique taxonomy are two choices the field rarely varies---how a detector \emph  {learns} as the system changes (static, periodic or online) and whether it is \emph  {evaluated} under drift---which \S  \ref {sec:problem} makes the explicit axes of this study.}}{7}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:taxonomy}{{1}{7}{A taxonomy of cloud-native anomaly-detection approaches---from statistical and threshold rules and classical machine learning, through the deep-learning families (reconstruction, sequential and graph models), to LLM-based and multimodal MELT-fusion detectors---with representative works. The strongest recent results cluster at the multimodal end (shaded). Cutting \emph {across} this technique taxonomy are two choices the field rarely varies---how a detector \emph {learns} as the system changes (static, periodic or online) and whether it is \emph {evaluated} under drift---which \S \ref {sec:problem} makes the explicit axes of this study}{figure.1}{}}
